V8 adaptive speculative decoding feasibility (Qwen3.5-4B-Q8 CUDA, RTX 5050 laptop 8 GB at 40 W)

GATES (set before any change)
 S0  correctness: speculative greedy output is byte-identical to plain decoding; the cache (keys, values, DeltaNet state)
     after acceptance, rejection, cancellation and rollback equals a plain run's, bit for bit
 S1  cost: one pass over k tokens (k <= 8) costs C(k); speculation can only pay if tokens delivered per pass
     exceed C(k)/C(1) by >= 10% once rollback is included. Measured before any proposal is judged
 S2  benefit: >= +10% decode throughput (or completion latency) on at least one realistic workload class
     (code edit, repetitive text, long-context chat) with identical output
 S3  harm: with the controller off or the guess rejected, <= 2% slower than plain decoding on every other class
 S4  memory: VRAM overhead bounded and inside the planner's slack (no new admission rule); host marks, batching,
     checkpoints and low-VRAM behaviour unchanged
 S5  separate draft model: only if S1 allows it and 0.6B-class weights + KV fit beside the 4.35 GB target
 Any gate failed -> NO-GO, report why, stop. No forced speedup, no new kernels, no scheduler rewrite.

AUDIT (before any change)
 Implemented: n-gram "guessing" in runtime::generate (Drafter over prompt + reply, match_len/max_draft, check of the guesses
   in one forward_rows pass, then kv.truncate over the rejected ones; sampler runs once per delivered token). Opt-in
   `--speculate` (marin-run, marin chat); server passes None. Qwen3 GPU: max_rows 8, truncate is a length. CPU, Granite,
   Qwen3.5 CPU and Qwen3.5 CUDA: max_rows 0 -> never guessed (archive 03: "state contracts not validated").
 Qwen3.5 CUDA could not check several tokens at once with per-row logits, and truncate() meant "read the whole history
   again" (recurrent state), so rejection cost a full replay. Draft-model speculation: measured no-go on Qwen3 (archive 01 s15).
 Existing tools reused: pass-cost, guess, guess-report, spec-bench (interleaved plain/spec runs, tokens compared, power read).

COST OF CHECKING k TOKENS (pass-cost, Qwen3.5-4B-Q8, median ms; ratio to one token)
 ctx      1             2            3            4            6             8
 128   24.42 1.00x  25.98 1.06x  27.84 1.14x  30.27 1.24x  63.58 2.60x  78.85 3.23x
 1024  24.77 1.00x  27.23 1.10x  29.95 1.21x  33.15 1.34x  65.44 2.64x  80.44 3.25x
 4096  26.04 1.00x  31.66 1.22x  34.65 1.33x  38.41 1.48x  72.86 2.80x  89.60 3.44x
 Up to 4 tokens cost 1.06-1.48x; from 6 tokens a different kernel costs 2.6-3.4x. So at most 4 rows (3 guesses).

REPLAY OF REAL REPLIES (24 prompts x 300 tokens, Qwen3.5's own greedy replies; sim.py prices a rejection as the
 failed pass + a re-read of the kept tokens + marks): rule 4 matching tokens, 3 guesses ->
   chat 0.99x, code_edit 2.01x, repetitive 1.37x, long_context 1.00x   (rule 2 matching: chat 0.97x, long 0.91x)
 The old Qwen3 rule (8 matching, 7 guesses) would need 8 rows (3.2x a pass): not used. New: Speculation::SHORT_PASS.

WHAT WAS BUILT (the minimum; no new kernels, scheduler, draft model or controller)
 - Qwen35Gpu::forward_rows: one pass over <= 4 tokens with the LM head on every row (the batched pass already did this);
   keeps the mark taken before it. MAX_ROWS = 4. CausalLm::speculation() picks the rule per model.
 - GpuCache::truncate inside the last rows pass no longer starts the history over: it keeps that mark as `resume`; the next
   pass puts the state back to it, reads only the kept tokens in a pass of their own (<= 4 rows), takes a fresh mark and checks
   the new guesses. A cut anywhere else is the old full re-read. Marks refuse a cache that is waiting to be read again.
 - A cut inside the rows pass leaves every older mark valid, so a chat boundary mark still returns the cache after a
   speculative reply (test); a cut below a mark still refuses it.
 First measurement of this design found the bug that mattered: the pass after a cut had no mark of its own, so the next
 rejection re-read the whole history (code_edit 0.46-0.53x, p95 gap 87 ms). Reading the tail first, then marking, fixed it.

