 class        tok/s plain -> spec   paired range   tokens/pass  identical (greedy)  J/token plain -> spec
 code_edit     39.9 -> 81.2   2.04x   1.76-2.24x        2.94        8/8            1.00 -> 0.49
 repetitive    40.0 -> 55.1   1.38x   1.00-1.70x        1.50        8/8            1.00 -> 0.73
 divergent*    40.4 -> 44.8   1.11x   1.05-1.20x        1.18       12/12           0.99 -> 0.89
 chat          40.0 -> 40.7   1.02x   1.00-1.04x        1.03        8/8            1.00 -> 0.98
 long_context  37.6 -> 37.7   1.00x   1.00-1.01x        0.99        8/8            1.06 -> 1.06
 (* prompts built so an earlier prefix matches and the continuation differs: "every line starts with ...")
 Sampled (T 0.7, top-k 20, top-p 0.8, fixed seed): code_edit 2.01x 8/8, repetitive 1.38x 8/8, divergent 1.11x 10/12 identical.
 Isolated re-run after the last edit (2 prompts per class, nothing else on the GPU): code_edit 1.88x (1.77-2.02), divergent 1.12x, 2/2 identical.
 Power 39.9-40.0 W in every mode. First token and prefill are untouched (speculation starts after the prompt pass).
 Completion of a 300-token code edit: about 7.5 s -> 3.7 s. Greedy output identical in 64/64 runs after the last fix.
 Process VRAM peak 4,440 MiB with and without (1.5k prompt + 150 tokens); the kept mark is +53 MB resident. Host RAM: none.

CORRECTNESS
 - A pass of 2-4 rows equals feeding the tokens one at a time to within float rounding (4.5e-8 on the tiny model), not to the bit,
   exactly like a prompt read in a different chunking. So SAMPLED output can differ from plain decoding when a draw falls
   within rounding of a probability boundary: 2 of 28 sampled runs on the real model. Greedy was identical every time.
 - Unit tests (gpu_rows_tests): rows vs one-at-a-time; a cut at every kept count 0-4 equals a cache that read only what was
   kept, bit for bit; seven cuts in a row never re-read the history; a pass that fails after a cut leaves a cache that reads
   everything again and recovers; a boundary mark taken before a reply still returns the cache; a cut below a mark refuses it.
 - speculate_qwen35.rs (real engine, tiny model, 3 prompts x greedy/sampled x 3 lengths x 3 rules): same tokens, stop and
   cache (next-token logits within 1e-4); cancelling after 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 tokens in the middle of a burst leaves the cache
   plain decoding leaves.
 - Low VRAM: a second program took the GPU memory during generation (about 600 and 250 MiB left free); plain and
   speculative runs both finished with byte-identical output. Speculation adds no failure mode: it takes the same per-pass
   device mark plain decoding takes, and keeps it. Overlap of the two programs was not timestamped.
 - Concurrency: the server does not guess (speculation None), so batching, checkpoints and host marks are untouched; the
   supported path is one stream (`--speculate`). Not tested with concurrent requests because it is not supported there.

CONTROLLER: not built. The rule (4 matching tokens, 3 guesses) already costs nothing where nothing repeats (chat 1.02x,
 long context 1.00x) and the worst measured case still gains (divergent 1.11x); the guesser takes 1.3 us per token. A
 live on/off controller has no measured loss to remove. Revisit only if a workload shows a speculative class below 0.98x.

DECISION: PARTIAL GO. Opt-in `--speculate` on Qwen3.5 CUDA for copy-heavy work (code edits, repeated text); never default,
 never in the server. Draft model: not built (archive 01 s15 measured it no-go; a 4-row check costs 1.2-1.5 passes, which
 n-gram copying already uses).
 Limits: sampled output is not guaranteed bit-identical; 4-row ceiling (3 guesses); no server support; GPU only.
